# Commands

`tabula [--format text|csv|json] [--kind inner|left] [--keep first|last] COMMAND ARGS...` reads CSV files (UTF-8, an optional byte order mark, LF or CRLF) and prints
a table. Every command returns a `Table`; `--format` says how it is printed (text by default). A mistake in a file or in a
request exits 1 with `tabula: <message>` on standard error; a command line that is not shaped right exits 2.
`--kind` and `--keep` are read by one command each (`join` and `dedupe`) but are accepted on any command line, before or after the
command, as `--opt value` or `--opt=value`; any other value is a usage error.

## show

`tabula show FILE` prints the whole table. Blank lines in the file are ignored. A row with more or fewer fields than the
header is a mistake that names the file and the line (`t.csv: line 3: expected 2 fields, found 1`).

## head

`tabula head FILE N` prints the header and the first N rows. N is a whole number (zero is fine); anything else is a usage
error.

## cols

`tabula cols FILE` prints one row per column with two columns, `position` (from 1) and `column`.

## apply

`tabula apply FILE COLUMN TRANSFORM...` runs each TRANSFORM, left to right, over every value of one column and prints the
whole table. A transform is `name` or `name:ARG`; the list is in `docs/transforms.md`. A value a transform cannot take is
a mistake that names the file and the row (`t.csv: row 2: 'abc' is not a number`). Rows count from 1, the header not
counted.

## sort

`tabula sort FILE KEY...` prints the rows sorted by the first KEY, ties by the second, and so on. A KEY is a column name,
optionally followed by the modifiers `:num` (compare as numbers, not as text) and `:desc` (largest first), in either order
(`price:num:desc`, `price:desc:num`). Text compares by code point, so `B` sorts before `a`. The sort is stable: rows that tie
on every key keep the order of the file, also with `:desc`. With `:num`, a cell that is not a number (an empty cell is not
one) is a mistake that names the file, the row in file order and the column (`t.csv: row 2: column 'price': 'abc' is not a
number`). An unknown modifier, a modifier given twice in one KEY, an empty column name or no KEY at all is a usage error.

## filter

`tabula filter FILE CONDITION...` prints the rows where every CONDITION holds, in the order of the file. A CONDITION is one
argument shaped `COLUMN OP VALUE` with no spaces added: `price>=10`, `city==Leeds`, `name~gnome`. The operators are `==`,
`!=`, `>=`, `<=`, `>`, `<` and `~` (the cell contains VALUE, ignoring upper and lower case; an empty VALUE matches every
row). The operator is the first one that appears in the argument, so a column name cannot contain one of them; where two
start at the same place the longer one wins (`>=` and not `>`). For every operator but `~`, when both the cell and VALUE are
numbers they are compared as numbers (`10` is greater than `9`, `1.0` equals `1`); otherwise as text, by code point. VALUE
may be empty (`city==` matches the rows whose city is empty). A table with no row left still prints its header. An argument
with no operator, nothing before the operator, or no CONDITION at all is a usage error; a column the file does not have is a
mistake (`t.csv: no column 'zip' (columns: name, price)`).
